    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_2c62141dd5e50906487c764216c36ba520ace95e5c2267950562e4d0dba9c961",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "All three calculations suppress detailed configurations at main order in favor of second-order or even-multiplicity structure, but by different operations: cumulant exponentiation with variance dominance gives Gaussian-leading moments, square support and multiplicity regrouping give a one-sided exponential bound, and unique factorization turns balanced distinct-prime products into permutation pairings. Their common output can be compared without identifying the mechanisms or underlying probability laws.",
