    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_92064ec467b9fff72da0a660cb26b3378e213dcd6435b3736c8ba1ff12348e0e",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Across several distinct scale limits, covariance decompositions separate shared low-frequency fluctuation from residual local structure. In the randomized zeta model a growing spatially constant Gaussian mode exponentiates into a scalar mass multiplying strict-logarithmic chaos; a Brownian-block coupling compares discrete CUE modes with a continuum chaos field; finite-dimensional zeta shifts realize minimum covariance as shared Brownian ancestry plus independent disorder; and microscopic CUE logarithms split a common rank-one fluctuation from bounded local geometry.",
